A fleet (`*`) `service call` whose procedure nobody could establish is
refused unless `--i-know` (#505).

RFC 05 §2.1 refuses a fan-out write three times over, and the registry layer
is the one a dynamic caller has: a `*` call to a procedure whose slice
declares `fanout = "forbidden"` — or a write with no `fanout`, which RFC 08 §2
defaults to forbidden — never leaves the process. But that guard ran only
when it could *find* the declaration. `--no-validate` loads no slices, and a
degraded introspect sweep yields none, so in exactly the cases where nothing
was known the call fanned out to every origin. Not knowing the declaration
is not a licence (RFC 09 §5.1 O4).

With `--no-validate` there is no registry to wait for, so the refusal comes
before a session opens, and exits **2**:

```console
$ zenctl service call '*' netring reset --no-validate
? 2
Error: procedure netring/reset: has no registry loaded to declare it, so whether it is a write could not be established — RFC 08 §2 defaults a write to fanout = "forbidden", and not knowing the declaration is not a licence (RFC 09 §5.1 O4). A fleet (`*`) call to it is refused (RFC 05 §2.1): name one origin, or pass --i-know to fan it out anyway

```

`--i-know` is the one acknowledgement `service call` carries, and it moves
exactly this refusal: the call goes on to open its session (here the dead-bus
fixture, so it stops there, an act's 1).

```console
$ zenctl service call '*' netring reset --no-validate --i-know --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5
? 1
Error: failed to open the Zenoh session

Caused by:
    Unable to connect to any of [Single(tcp/127.0.0.1:1)]!

```

It does not move a write the convention itself declares. RFC 05 §5.1's table
makes every key under `config/<r>/` but the read-back a write, and a write
that does not say `fanout = "allowed"` may not fan out — with or without a
registry, with or without `--i-know`:

```console
$ zenctl service call '*' modem config/wlan0/link/set --no-validate --i-know
? 2
Error: procedure modem/config/wlan0/link/set: is a write by the convention's own table (RFC 05 §5.1), and a write with no declared fanout defaults to forbidden (RFC 08 §2) — a fleet (`*`) call to it is refused (RFC 05 §2.1); name one origin

```

And it is not needed for what the convention declares a read: `introspect`
(RFC 08 §6), `describe` (§7) and a configuration read-back fan out with no
registry at all, which is what `config get '*'` and `bench rpc '*'` stand on.

```console
$ zenctl service call '*' netring introspect --no-validate --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5
? 1
Error: failed to open the Zenoh session

Caused by:
    Unable to connect to any of [Single(tcp/127.0.0.1:1)]!

```
